    "id": "p57-q18-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "18",
    "prompt": "Solve the following. $\\dfrac2{x+1}+\\dfrac3{x-2}=\\dfrac{10}{x^2-x-2}$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$x=\\dfrac{11}{5}$; original domain $x\\ne-1,2$.\n\nFactorise $x^2-x-2=(x+1)(x-2)$ and clear the common denominator.",
      "worked": "Since $x^2-x-2=(x+1)(x-2)$, the original domain is $x\\ne-1,2$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{2(x-2)+3(x+1)}{(x+1)(x-2)}&=\\frac{10}{(x+1)(x-2)}\\\\2(x-2)+3(x+1)&=10\\\\2x-4+3x+3&=10\\\\5x-1&=10\\\\5x&=11\\\\x&=\\frac{11}{5}.\\end{align*}$$\nThis value is in the original domain.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 28
  },
